  "concubinageDetailFiscal": "DÉTAIL FISCAL",
  "concubinageImpots2Celibataires": "Taxes as 2 single persons",
  "concubinageImpotsMaries": "Impôts mariés",
  "concubinageEcartImpotMariePlusEleve": "Household tax higher when married",
  "concubinageEcartImpotConcubinagePlusEleve": "Household tax higher when cohabiting",
  "impositionCommuneMecanique": "Once married, both incomes are added together and taxed jointly, under a reduced scale reserved for couples. Which way the gap goes depends on how the incomes are split: when they are close, adding them pushes taxable income up faster than the reduced scale brings it down — this is the effect known as « Heiratsstrafe ». When one income dominates, that same scale brings the household tax below what two single people would pay.",
  "fiscalEstimationModelLimit": "Simplified estimate: it accounts for neither your actual deductions, nor your municipality, nor the detailed cantonal scale. The real gap may therefore differ, including in its direction.",
  "concubinageImpotSuccession": "INHERITANCE TAX",
  "concubinageMarieExonere": "0% (exempt)",
  "concubinageConcubinLabel": "Cohabiting partner",
  "concubinageConcubinTaux": "Taxed at the “third party” rate",
  "concubinageNeutralTitle": "No option is universally suited",
  "concubinageNeutralDesc": "The choice between marriage and cohabitation depends on your situation: income, assets, children, canton, life plans. Marriage offers more automatic legal protections, cohabitation more flexibility. A specialist can help you see more clearly.",
  "concubinageChecklistIntro": "In cohabitation, nothing is automatic. Here are the essential protections to put in place to protect your partner.",
  "concubinageProtectionsCount": "{count}/{total} protections in place",
  "@concubinageProtectionsCount": {
    "placeholders": {
      "count": {
        "type": "int"
      },
      "total": {
        "type": "int"
      }
    }
  },
  "concubinageChecklist1Title": "Draft a will",
  "concubinageChecklist1Desc": "Without a will, your partner inherits nothing — your estate follows the legal order (first your descendants, otherwise your parents, then your siblings). A holographic will (handwritten, dated, signed) is enough; you can bequeath the available portion to your partner.",
  "concubinageChecklist2Title": "LPP beneficiary clause",
  "concubinageChecklist2Desc": "Contact your pension fund to register your partner as beneficiary. Without this clause, the LPP death capital does not go to them. Most funds accept the cohabitant under conditions (shared household, etc.).",
  "concubinageChecklist3Title": "Cohabitation agreement",
  "concubinageChecklist3Desc": "A written contract that governs the sharing of expenses, property ownership, and what happens in case of separation. Not mandatory, but strongly recommended — especially if you buy property together.",
  "concubinageChecklist4Title": "Cross life insurance",
  "concubinageChecklist4Desc": "A life insurance where each partner is the other's beneficiary compensates for the absence of AVS/LPP survivor's pension. Compare offers — premiums depend on age and insured capital.",
  "concubinageChecklist5Title": "Advance directive for incapacity",
  "concubinageChecklist5Desc": "If you become incapacitated (accident, illness), your partner has no power of representation. An advance directive for incapacity (CC art. 360 ff.) grants them this right.",
  "concubinageChecklist6Title": "Advance directives",
  "concubinageChecklist6Desc": "A document specifying your medical wishes in case of incapacity. You can designate your partner as the trusted person for medical decisions (CC art. 370 ff.).",
  "concubinageChecklist7Title": "Joint account for shared expenses",
  "concubinageChecklist7Desc": "A joint account simplifies managing shared expenses (rent, groceries, bills). Clearly define each person's contribution. In case of separation, the balance is split 50/50 unless agreed otherwise.",
  "concubinageChecklist8Title": "Joint or individual lease",
  "concubinageChecklist8Desc": "If you are on the lease with your partner, you are jointly liable. In case of separation, both must give notice. If only one is the leaseholder, the other has no rights to the housing.",
  "concubinageDisclaimer": "Simplified information for educational purposes — does not constitute legal or tax advice. Rules depend on the canton, municipality and your personal situation. Consult a legal specialist for personalised advice.",
